  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a conceptual map from particle-physics gauge structure to quantum-computing implementations. At the top, the three parts of the Standard Model gauge symmetry, U(1), SU(2), and SU(3), are shown through increasingly rich state spaces: simple charge-like labels for U(1), spin or isospin multiplets for SU(2), and a larger network of coupled representations for SU(3). A second schematic connects these abstract fields to quark content, with a central interaction node linked to quark–antiquark pairs and flavor states such as up and down, suggesting how field degrees of freedom can be encoded in quantum states.\n\nThese ingredients feed into a central Hamiltonian expression containing gauge and fermionic terms, indicating the target dynamics to be simulated. Below, a curved blue grid resembles an energy or configuration landscape. On this surface sit quantum-circuit icons, numbered plaquette-like loops, and linked blue nodes, which appear to represent encoded lattice sites, interactions, and gate sequences used to realize the model. A Gauss-law constraint is highlighted, emphasizing restriction to physical states only. Overall, the image conveys a pipeline from non-Abelian gauge theory and quark interactions to constrained lattice encoding and eventual quantum computation.",
